-- Q679: Stock Compensation and Business Combinations [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 718-10-25-2, restricted stock represents actual shares granted to the employee with no exercise price required. The shares are subject to forfeiture if vesting conditions are not met, and fair value equals the grant-date market price because employees receive the full value upon vesting.
Wrong (B): Restricted stock is not always more valuable; its value depends on the grant-date stock price, while options may be more valuable if the stock appreciates significantly above the exercise price.
Wrong (C): Restricted stock is within the scope of ASC 718 and requires compensation expense recognition over the vesting period.
Wrong (D): ASC 718 requires recognition of compensation expense for restricted stock based on grant-date fair value over the requisite service period.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 679;

-- Q683: Stock Compensation and Business Combinations [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 805-30-25, when the fair value of identifiable net assets ($9,200,000) exceeds the purchase price ($8,000,000), a bargain purchase occurs. After reassessing that all assets and liabilities are properly identified and measured, the $1,200,000 excess is recognized as a gain in earnings on the acquisition date.
Wrong (A): ASC 805 eliminated the concept of recording negative goodwill as a liability; the excess is recognized as a gain immediately.
Wrong (B): Reducing non-current asset fair values is not permitted; the acquirer must use actual fair values, not artificially reduce them to eliminate the excess.
Wrong (D): Deferring and amortizing the excess over 10 years was an approach under prior standards but is no longer allowed under ASC 805.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 683;

-- Q684: Stock Compensation and Business Combinations [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 805-30-35, contingent consideration classified as a liability is remeasured at fair value each reporting period after the measurement period ends, with changes recognized in earnings. During the measurement period (up to one year), adjustments reflecting facts existing at acquisition date are recorded against goodwill.
Wrong (A): Liability-classified contingent consideration requires ongoing fair value remeasurement; freezing it at initial recognition ignores subsequent changes in expected outcomes.
Wrong (B): After the measurement period, changes in fair value are recorded in earnings, not as adjustments to goodwill.
Wrong (D): Contingent consideration is not a fixed obligation amortized on a straight-line basis; it is a variable fair-value liability remeasured each period.$EXPL$
