-- Q701: Advanced Consolidations [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 810-10-40-3A, when a parent loses control of a subsidiary (ownership drops below 50%), full deconsolidation is required. The subsidiary''s assets, liabilities, and NCI are removed, and any retained noncontrolling interest is recognized at fair value. A gain or loss is calculated on the deconsolidation date.
Wrong (A): Consolidation requires control; at 25% ownership, Aldrich no longer controls Devlin and must deconsolidate, not continue consolidating at the reduced level.
Wrong (C): An impairment loss applies to asset write-downs, not to loss-of-control transactions, which are accounted for as disposals with gain or loss recognition.
Wrong (D): Deconsolidation is prospective from the date control is lost; prior-year financials are not retroactively restated.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 701;

-- Q702: Advanced Consolidations [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 810-10-45-19, in an upstream intercompany sale the subsidiary initiates the transaction, so the NCI bears its proportionate share of the unrealized profit elimination. Cedar''s 20% NCI absorbs $24,000 of the $120,000 unrealized profit because NCI shares in the subsidiary''s adjusted earnings.
Wrong (A): NCI does bear a share because the subsidiary originated the upstream sale, and NCI participates in the subsidiary''s earnings and adjustments.
Wrong (C): Assigning 100% of the elimination to NCI is incorrect; 100% attribution to one party applies only in downstream sales (attributed to the parent, not NCI).
Wrong (D): Intercompany transactions directly affect NCI when the subsidiary is the selling entity; NCI is not insulated from upstream profit eliminations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 702;

-- Q703: Advanced Consolidations [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 810-10-15-14, an entity with insufficient equity at risk to finance its activities without additional subordinated support is a VIE. SPEs created for securitization typically have minimal equity and a single party absorbing the majority of losses, making them a common VIE structure requiring consolidation.
Wrong (A): A publicly traded subsidiary with minority shareholders is evaluated under the voting interest model, not the VIE model, because it has sufficient equity at risk.
Wrong (C): A 50/50 joint venture typically has adequate equity from both partners and is not structured as a VIE unless it meets the insufficient-equity criteria.
Wrong (D): A wholly owned subsidiary is consolidated under the voting interest model through 100% ownership, not through the VIE framework.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 703;
